  "content": {
    "title": "Here's what to know about they psychedelic drug called ibogaine",
    "text": "A general view of a jar filled with finely ground iboga powder made from the powerful psychoactive root.NAO MUKADI/AFP via Getty ImagesPresident Donald Trump on Saturday signed an executive order aimed at expanding research into a psychedelic drug called ibogaine — an effort Texas has joined. The move to ease restrictions into researching the drug are part of a broader push for scientists to study the therapeutic benefits of LSD, ecstasy and other mind-altering drugs. Ibogaine, a powerful psychedelic, has garnered interest for its potential to treat opioid-use disorder and other forms of - abuse. Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Texas leaders have also expressed interest in having researchers look at the potential benefits of psychedelic drugs for mental health treatments. Last year, Gov. Greg Abbott signed a law designating $50 million in funds for ibogaine treatment research.Article continues below this adHere's what to know about ibogaine and why lawmakers are easing restrictions.Ibogaine is a psychedelic medicine derived from the roots of the African iboga plant. It is classified as a Schedule I-controlled substance — meaning it has a high abuse risk and no acceptable medical use.What does ibogaine do?While the drug is known for its hallucinogenic and dream-inducing effects, researchers have found that it could have a positive effect on key neurotransmitters related to moods in humans such as serotonin and dopamine.Article continues below this adResearchers at Stanford University found that the drug reduced the psychiatric symptoms of veterans with traumatic brain injuries, including depression, potential suicide and post-traumatic stress disorder.Some claim that the psychedelic experience created by ibogaine could be beneficial in the treatment of substance-use disorders, with research suggesting that this altered state may alleviate symptoms of withdrawal and help curb cravings.Does it have side effects?The University of California Berkeley Center for the Science of Psychedelics said taking ibogaine could result in fatal cardiac effects. Other possible side effects include nausea, tremors, psychosis, mania and seizures.But a Stanford University study in which researchers treated a group of veterans with ibogaine found that none of the participants experienced serious side effects other than headaches and nausea.Why did Trump ease restrictions on ibogaine?The president's executive order directs the Food and Drug Administration, the Drug Enforcement Administration, the Department of Justice and other federal agencies to reduce the red tape and restrictions on psychedelic compounds that have made it difficult for researchers to study them.Trump's move paves the way for scientists to look into the potential benefits of ibogaine and other psychedelic drugs as a growing number of studies suggest that psychedelic compounds can be effective in treating a range of mental health challenges and substance-use disorders.The order also aims to expand participation in clinical trials for ibogaine and other drugs by directing federal agencies to collaborate with the Department of Veterans Affairs, which is already funding research into psychedelic-assisted therapies for veterans with PTSD, depression and alcohol-use disorder.Why is Texas investing in it?While state lawmakers are interested in exploring ibogaine's potential benefits for treating veterans in Texas, they're also interested in reaping the financial benefits of the drug.State officials previously sought out to establish public-private partnerships with drug companies to develop ibogaine into a drug for FDA approval and for pharmacies to sell. Through that collaboration, the Texas government would have gotten a portion of whatever revenues they earned from ibogaine.However, after failing to find any companies to work with them, the state announced it would launch its own research program. Texas Health and Human Services selected UTHealth Houston, in collaboration with the University of Texas Medical Branch at Galveston, to help conduct a two-year research trial evaluating the effect of ibogaine on patients with addiction, traumatic brain injury, and other behavioral health conditions.Start your day and stay informed with essential Houston news.",
